{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"$h$ is the derivative of $H$, which means $H$ is an antiderivative of $h$.\n\nSince we know the antiderivative of $h$, we can use the fundamental theorem of calculus: For every function $h$ and its antiderivative $H$,\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int_a^b h(x)\\,dx=H(b)-H(a)$.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\u0026\\phantom{=}\\displaystyle\\int_{-6}^{-2} h(x)\\,dx\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=H(\\blueD{-2})-H(\\blueD{-6})\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\left|\\dfrac{\\blueD{-2}}{2}\\right|-\\left|\\dfrac{\\blueD{-6}}{2}\\right|\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=1-3\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-2\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion,\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int_{-6}^{-2} h(x)\\,dx=-2$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\u0026H(x)=\\left|\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right|\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026h(x)=H'(x)\n\\end{align}$\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int_{-6}^{-2} h(x)\\,dx=$ [[☃ numeric-input 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"numeric-input 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answers":[{"maxError":null,"message":"","simplify":"required","status":"correct","strict":false,"value":-2}],"coefficient":false,"labelText":"definite integral","size":"normal","static":false},"static":false,"type":"numeric-input","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}}}
